course: dictionary
lesson: 1972
title: Complete: finish, whole or total
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Complete has more than one meaning, so the context tells you which one fits. «Electricity flows only around a complete circuit.» Here complete means whole, with every part joined up, because a loop with a gap does not work.
explain: As a verb, to complete something is to finish all of it. «Please complete the table» means fill in every box. The word completed tells you the job is done, and completing means you are still working on it.
explain: Complete can also mean total, as in «It was a complete surprise.» Ask: what word would fit here? Try whole, finish or total in the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
fact: Complete can be a verb (complete, completes, completed, completing) or an adjective.
fact: To complete something is to finish all of it.
fact: Complete means whole, with every part, as in «a complete set».
fact: Complete means total, as in «a complete surprise».
fact: To complete a form is to fill it in.
exercise: «Electricity flows only around a complete circuit.» What does complete mean here? | *Whole, with every part joined | Lost | Half finished | Very quick
exercise: «Please complete the table.» What does complete mean here? | Throw it away | Copy it onto a new page | Read it aloud | *Fill in every box
exercise: «It was a complete surprise.» What does complete mean here? | Small | *Total | Late | Cheap
test: Ella wants to complete her maths homework before tea. What does complete mean? | Copy it from a friend | Throw it away | Start it again | *Finish all of it
test: «A run-on sentence joins two or more complete sentences with no proper punctuation.» What does complete mean here? | Loud | *Whole, with every part | Unfinished | Very short
exam: «Ravi gave a complete answer, with every step shown.» What does complete mean here? | *Whole, with every part | Torn in half | Given at the end of the day | Said quietly
